Subject: RFC: message queue retry policy
Message-ID: <thread1-01@example.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Feb 2026 13:00:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Team,

Our current retry policy for failed SMTP deliveries is:

  Retry 1: immediate
  Retry 2: 5 minutes
  Retry 3: 30 minutes
  Retry 4: 2 hours
  Retry 5: give up, bounce

I think we should extend this. Most major MTAs retry for 4-5 days
before bouncing. Proposed new policy:

  Retry 1: immediate
  Retry 2: 5 minutes
  Retry 3: 30 minutes
  Retry 4: 2 hours
  Retry 5: 8 hours
  Retry 6: 24 hours
  Retry 7: 48 hours
  Retry 8: 72 hours
  Retry 9: give up, bounce (total: ~5 days)

The queue worker already supports configurable backoff. We just
need to update the defaults and add a config option.

Thoughts?

- Alice

From bob@example.com Mon Feb 23 14:15:00 2026
From: Bob Martinez <bob@example.com>
To: alice@example.com, charlie@example.com
Subject: Re: RFC: message queue retry policy
Message-ID: <thread1-02@example.com>
In-Reply-To: <thread1-01@example.com>
References: <thread1-01@example.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Feb 2026 14:15:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Alice,

The extended retry policy makes sense. Two concerns:

1. Storage: each queued message holds a blob reference. With 5-day
   retries, we could accumulate a lot of queue entries during an
   outage. Should we add a max queue size per user?

2. Notifications: should we send the sender a "delayed delivery"
   notification after retry 4 or 5? Postfix does this and it's
   helpful for users who don't realize the recipient's server is
   down.

Otherwise +1 on the proposal.

- Bob

From charlie@example.com Mon Feb 23 15:30:00 2026
From: Charlie Wu <charlie@example.com>
To: alice@example.com, bob@example.com
Subject: Re: RFC: message queue retry policy
Message-ID: <thread1-03@example.com>
In-Reply-To: <thread1-02@example.com>
References: <thread1-01@example.com> <thread1-02@example.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Feb 2026 15:30:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Both good points. On Bob's items:

1. Max queue size: yes, but per-domain rather than per-user. If
   gmail.com is down, all users sending to Gmail will queue up.
   Per-domain cap of 10,000 messages seems reasonable.

2. Delayed delivery notification: +1. RFC 3461 (DSN) defines the
   "delayed" action value for this exact purpose. We should
   implement it properly rather than rolling our own format.

One more thing: we should also implement the SMTP ENHANCEDSTATUSCODES
extension (RFC 2034) so receiving servers know we understand DSN.

- Charlie

