From charlie@example.com Thu Feb 26 10:00:00 2026
From: Charlie Wu <charlie@example.com>
To: alice@example.com, bob@example.com
Subject: Raft snapshot size investigation
Message-ID: <thread3-01@example.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Feb 2026 10:00:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Team,

I've been investigating why Raft snapshots are growing so large.
Current snapshot for a test cluster with 1,000 users:

  Pebble data: 2.1 GB
  Raft snapshot: 3.4 GB (62% overhead!)

The problem: we're serializing the entire Pebble state as-is into
the snapshot. Pebble's internal format includes tombstones, bloom
filters, and multiple SSTable levels that are redundant in a
snapshot context.

Proposed fix: instead of dumping raw Pebble data, iterate all
live key-value pairs and write them as a flat protobuf stream.
This should bring snapshot size close to actual data size.

- Charlie

From alice@example.com Thu Feb 26 11:00:00 2026
From: Alice Chen <alice@example.com>
To: charlie@example.com, bob@example.com
Subject: Re: Raft snapshot size investigation
Message-ID: <thread3-02@example.com>
In-Reply-To: <thread3-01@example.com>
References: <thread3-01@example.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Feb 2026 11:00:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Charlie,

Good catch. 62% overhead is too much, especially for clusters
with large mailboxes.

The flat protobuf stream approach should work. A few things:

1. Compress the stream with zstd -- Pebble data compresses well
   (typically 3-4x ratio for email metadata)
2. Add a header with version and key count so the receiver can
   pre-allocate and show progress
3. Stream the snapshot rather than buffering in memory -- we don't
   want to OOM on large datasets

With zstd compression, I'd expect the 2.1 GB of live data to
compress to ~600 MB. That's a 5.6x improvement over current.

- Alice

From bob@example.com Thu Feb 26 14:30:00 2026
From: Bob Martinez <bob@example.com>
To: alice@example.com, charlie@example.com
Subject: Re: Raft snapshot size investigation
Message-ID: <thread3-03@example.com>
In-Reply-To: <thread3-02@example.com>
References: <thread3-01@example.com> <thread3-02@example.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Feb 2026 14:30:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Alice's estimates match my experience with zstd on similar data.

One concern: during snapshot streaming, Pebble needs to maintain
a consistent view. We should use a Pebble snapshot (the database
kind, not Raft kind) to get a point-in-time read view without
blocking writes.

The HashiCorp Raft library's FSM interface has a Snapshot() method
that returns an FSMSnapshot. We control the Persist() method on
that, so we can iterate using a Pebble snapshot and stream+compress
there.

I'll sketch out the FSMSnapshot implementation if Charlie wants
to handle the protobuf stream format.

- Bob

From charlie@example.com Fri Feb 27 09:00:00 2026
From: Charlie Wu <charlie@example.com>
To: alice@example.com, bob@example.com
Subject: Re: Raft snapshot size investigation
Message-ID: <thread3-04@example.com>
In-Reply-To: <thread3-03@example.com>
References: <thread3-01@example.com> <thread3-02@example.com> <thread3-03@example.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Feb 2026 09:00:00 +0000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Sounds like a plan. I'll define the protobuf message:

message SnapshotHeader {
  uint32 version = 1;
  uint64 key_count = 2;
  uint64 term = 3;
  uint64 index = 4;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp created_at = 5;
}

message SnapshotEntry {
  bytes key = 1;
  bytes value = 2;
}

The stream format will be:
  [header length varint][SnapshotHeader]
  [entry length varint][SnapshotEntry] (repeated)

Bob, I'll have the proto file ready today so you can start on
the FSMSnapshot implementation.

- Charlie

